 "notes": "Printed p.155, Table XV Part II Bi-lingualism: Gwalior State, Gird, Bhind, Tonwarghar, Sheopur, Narwar districts; continued on p.156 (leaf 160). Record with subsidiary=null = number speaking the mother tongue (cols 3-5); other records = males/females also speaking the subsidiary language. Mother-tongue rows are printed only where the district has entries (e.g. no Bhil Dialects row for Bhind, Tonwarghar, Sheopur). In the subsidiary block the figures drift downward relative to the row labels (up to about one line in the lower districts); rows assigned by order, and every district's subsidiary columns add to its Total row except as listed. Faint marginal figures ('1','2','4'... at the left edge of col 6) are show-through/clipped digits from the facing page, not data. Several 3/8 and 5/6 ambiguities resolved by P=M+F and by the Total rows: State Rajasthani P 1,082,132 (the 8 looks like 3), State Western Hindi M 1,234,173 (last digit looks like 8), Gird Western Hindi M 189,238, Narwar Western Hindi P 400,213, Narwar Rajasthani-MT x Western Hindi F 143 (last digit 3/8 ambiguous; Total 266 requires 143), State Marathi-MT x Western Hindi F 4,383 (second digit broken; district figures sum to 4,383 and 4,883 would break the Total 50,004). PRINT INCONSISTENCIES kept as printed: (1) State Total x Rajasthani F printed 21,093 but its rows sum 1,741+18,205+1,840+307 = 22,093 (the 21,093 equals the sum of the district Total rows, including Amjhera's 2,126, which is itself ~1,000 below its own rows). (2) State Total x Marathi M 2,414 = district totals, but rows sum 100+2,318+1 = 2,419; the districts' Western Hindi-MT x Marathi M sum to 2,313, so 2,318 (clearly an 8 at zoom) looks like a misprint for 2,313. (3) State Bhil Dialects-MT x Rajasthani F 1,741 vs the only district entry (Amjhera, read 1,711 but uncertain) — see leaf 160. State = sum of the 11 districts holds for every other cell checked (P/M/F of all mother tongues, all subsidiary cells)."
